# ML and evaluation rules

- Establish a simple baseline before adding complexity.
- Evaluate B0, B1 and B2 on identical sample IDs and conditions.
- Use validation data for threshold selection; reserve test data for final evaluation.
- Report sample counts and denominators with every metric.
- Implement metrics from saved per-sample predictions, not manually typed values.
- Unit-test WER, intent accuracy, slot precision/recall/F1 and exact-frame matching on hand-checkable toy cases.
- A model score or decoder confidence is not a calibrated probability unless calibration has been evaluated.
- Do not call generated transcript variants genuine ASR N-best hypotheses.
- Record the true provenance of every candidate.
- Do not call known injected corruption an online root-cause diagnosis.
- Keep `injected_failure_type` separate from `predicted_failure_type`.
- Synthetic corruption is a stress-test condition, not evidence of natural failure frequency.
- Include clean-condition false-positive measurements.
- Report failed runs, exclusions and missing outputs.
- Do not quietly drop difficult samples.
- Compare latency with hardware, batch size, warm-up and timing method documented.
